The libcrc profile is almost entirely one loop: crc_32::byte_loop, the table-driven reflected CRC-32 update, at 99.8% of cycles. The Amdahl ceiling is therefore about 600x, and covering this one kernel is enough. I designed one memory-operand instruction, crc32.step64b rd, rs1, rs2. It is encoded on custom-1 with funct3=000 and funct7=0000000, which keeps it clear of red_accel's custom-0 slot and of the mul/div opcodes. Its 17-word block holds the running CRC state in in[0] and 64 little-endian data bytes in in[1..16], which are the RV32 memory image of the data. It writes the updated state to out[0], zeroes out[1..16] and returns 0 in rd. This is the 17-word, 64-byte shape that already shipped in converged runs. The 28-cycle issue cost plus 34 words of traffic is spread over 64 bytes, compared with about 7 host instructions (about 60 target cycles) per byte in software. invocations=524 comes from data volume: 234,634 host instructions per call at 7 per byte is 33,518 bytes per call, which is the average of the 64 KiB buffer and the 1500 B frame. Those calls need 1024 and 23 64-byte steps. marshal_words=18 counts 1 state word plus 16 copied data words in and 1 result word out. The S2c critique found the instruction total and constant time: bit-serial with a mask select, fixed trip counts, no undefined shifts. It writes every output word, reads all input before writing so aliasing is defined, ignores address bits [1:0], and has no encoding collision. It uses no multiplier, so mac_ops=0 and it adds no second multiplier to the core.